  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A shortwave ejecting from a broader low\npressure circulation situated over the Gulf of Alaska moves into\nthe west coast today. Though this feature is expected to split as\nit moves inland with the main portion of energy moving south, \nskirting Nevada. No significant weather effects will be present\ntoday as a result. High pressure builds and establishes itself \noffshore of SoCal tonight and Thursday. This will keep generally \nzonal flow over the Great Basin and the storm track pushed to the \nnorth with activity directed into the PacNW and western Canada. \nThis keeps fair weather in place thru Friday under a warming \ntrend. By Friday, daytime high temperatures look to reside in the \nupper 40s to upper 50s, running around ten degrees warmer than \nnormal for this time of year. \n\nNumerical model solutions are beginning to come into better\nagreement with regard to chances of precipitaton this weekend. \nThe trend is favoring showers returning Friday evening in \nnorthern Nevada as a shortwave moves into the intermountain west \nfrom the northwest. Based on the indicated trajectory the best \nopportunity for shower activity will be within northern Elko \nCounty though any accumulation looks to remain less than 0.10 \ninches. Westerly winds will also be gusty Friday evening and into \nSaturday morning, particularly in and near Elko County, and gusts\nas high as 45 mph look likely. \n\nThe next Pacific storm system moves into the west coast Sunday, \ndisplacing the region of high pressure offshore of SoCal. Snow \nlevels are indicated to be around 6500 feet Sunday night. \nPrecipitation will be focused across northern Nevada though \naccumulation again looks modest with valleys observing around 0.10\ninches or less. Southwesterly winds will be elevated. Temperatures\nreturn to near to a few degrees warmer than seasonal values for \nthe weekend as well. Weather looks to remain unsettled into mid \nweek as another, likely stronger, Pacific system is indicated to \nmove into the west coast. High degree of uncertainty in the \ntiming of precipitation associated with this event is noted \nhowever. Rain and snow may move in as early as Tuesday morning or\nnot until Wednesday morning.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Confidence is high with regard\nto warmer than average temperatures and fair weather thru Friday.\nConfidence is moderate pertaining to increasing probability of\nprecipitaton this Friday evening and this weekend. Low confidence\npertaining to the weather event indicated for Tuesday/Wednesday\nnext week. Minimal deviations from NBM/WPC forecast.",
